1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,100
Ask an AI for a source,

2
00:00:02,100 --> 00:00:03,035
and it might give you

3
00:00:03,035 --> 00:00:04,560
one that doesn't exist.

4
00:00:04,560 --> 00:00:07,350
Title, author,
even page numbers.

5
00:00:07,350 --> 00:00:08,500
It isn't lying.

6
00:00:08,500 --> 00:00:10,630
It's doing what it
was built to do.

7
00:00:10,630 --> 00:00:13,380
Here's why AI sometimes
makes things up.

8
00:00:13,380 --> 00:00:14,557
A language model writes

9
00:00:14,557 --> 00:00:16,400
by predicting the next token,

10
00:00:16,400 --> 00:00:17,775
using patterns learned

11
00:00:17,775 --> 00:00:20,110
from huge amounts of text.

12
00:00:20,110 --> 00:00:21,505
Facts that appeared again

13
00:00:21,505 --> 00:00:23,660
and again leave strong patterns,

14
00:00:23,660 --> 00:00:26,090
so those answers
are usually right.

15
00:00:26,090 --> 00:00:28,550
But rare facts,
like a small study

16
00:00:28,550 --> 00:00:29,990
or an obscure date,

17
00:00:29,990 --> 00:00:31,850
leave weak patterns.

18
00:00:31,850 --> 00:00:34,170
The model still has
to write something,

19
00:00:34,170 --> 00:00:35,379
so it writes what a right

20
00:00:35,379 --> 00:00:37,360
answer would sound like.

21
00:00:37,360 --> 00:00:39,980
Researchers call
this a hallucination.

22
00:00:39,980 --> 00:00:43,030
And many tests used
to train and rank AI

23
00:00:43,030 --> 00:00:45,110
reward a confident guess over

24
00:00:45,110 --> 00:00:45,910
"I'm not sure. "

25
00:00:46,780 --> 00:00:47,974
Tools that search the web

26
00:00:47,974 --> 00:00:50,120
or your documents
first can help.

27
00:00:50,120 --> 00:00:52,300
But no AI is right every time.

28
00:00:52,300 --> 00:00:54,920
So use AI for ideas and drafts.

29
00:00:54,920 --> 00:00:56,500
For facts that matter,
check the source,

30
00:00:56,500 --> 00:00:57,490
and make sure it's real.
